From b2ee6b23ad50a2ab2eab158ea722cd3efa8d9f6c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Day <me@samcday.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 17:27:17 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 01/11] configuration: merge field-wise, search /run, read once

The configuration loader had three problems that matter as soon as more
than one file is involved:

- A later file with a [main] or [mass_storage] section that does not set
  a key reset it to None, so a drop-in adding an unrelated key silently
  dropped default_mode. Keys are now merged field-wise: a key that is set
  wins, an absent key never resets it.
- classic_system() searches /var/run rather than /run. That only works
  where /var/run is a symlink to /run and /var is already mounted. Search
  /usr/lib, /run and /etc literally, as UAPI.6 describes, so runtime
  configuration written by an initramfs is always found.
- Having no configuration file at all was treated as an error. It now
  means "use the defaults" and is logged as information.

The configuration is parsed once at startup into an immutable
Arc<Configuration> that remembers which file set each key, instead of
re-reading every file on each access. Any I/O, parse or validation error
(for example a default_mode that is not a known mode) marks the whole
configuration as broken: the offending file is logged, no value from any
file is used and no default mode is applied.

`usb-signaller --print-config` prints the merged configuration with the
source of each key and exits without touching D-Bus or USB, which makes
drop-in precedence easy to check on a device.

The search root can be moved for tests; the new tests cover precedence,
/dev/null masking, the merge fix, a missing configuration, a /run drop-in
without /var/run and broken files. Tokio's rt-multi-thread and sync
features are now declared explicitly instead of relying on zbus enabling
them.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
