--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -64,9 +64,35 @@ usb-signaller reads a TOML file to allow configuring several options, divided in
 usb-signaller complies with [UAPI 6](https://uapi-group.org/specifications/specs/configuration_files_specification/)
 allowing distros to provide a sane default but also users to change that default.
 
+Files are searched in `/usr/lib` (distro defaults), `/run` (runtime configuration,
+for example written by an initramfs) and `/etc` (administrator), in increasing
+order of precedence, and read in this order:
+
+1. The main file `usb-signaller/usb-signaller.toml`. Only the one with the
+   highest precedence is read: `/etc/usb-signaller/usb-signaller.toml` hides the
+   one in `/usr/lib`.
+2. Drop-ins `usb-signaller/usb-signaller.toml.d/*.toml` from all three
+   directories, sorted by file name. A drop-in replaces a lower precedence
+   drop-in with the same file name; a symlink to `/dev/null` masks it.
+3. Drop-ins `usb-signaller.d/*.toml`, with the same rules.
+
+Settings from later files override earlier ones key by key: a file that does
+not set a key never resets it. The configuration is read once at startup;
+restart usb-signaller to apply changes. Without any configuration file,
+usb-signaller runs with defaults.
+
+If any file cannot be read or parsed, or contains an invalid value, the whole
+configuration is considered broken: usb-signaller logs the file and the error,
+ignores every file and does not apply a default mode.
+
+`usb-signaller --print-config` prints the merged configuration with the file
+that set each key and exits without touching D-Bus or USB. It exits with status
+1 if the configuration is broken.
+
 ### Section main
 
 - **default_mode** (String): defines which USB mode must be applied on startup, for example: `developer_mode`.
+  Must be one of the USB modes below.
 
 ### Section mass\_storage
 
@@ -79,10 +105,11 @@ The following USB modes are available:
 - `charging_only`: no USB gadget is active, only charging is possible.
 - `developer_mode`: USB NCM ethernet gadget is active, only local IP traffic is allowed such SSHing into a device.
 - `tethering_mode`: USB NCM ethernet gadget is active, internet access from the device is shared.
-- `mtp_mode`: FunctionFS gadget is active, MTP daemon allows to exchange data over [Media Transfer Protocol](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Media_Transfer_Protocol).
+- `mtp_ffs_mode`: FunctionFS gadget is active, MTP daemon allows to exchange data over [Media Transfer Protocol](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Media_Transfer_Protocol).
 - `mass_storage_mode`: Mass Storage gadget is active, device acts as a storage device.
 - `cdrom_mode`: Mass Storage gadget is active, device acts as an inserted CD-ROM.
-- `accessory`: FunctionFS gadget is active, [Android Open Accessory](https://source.android.com/docs/core/interaction/accessories/protocol) ID vendor and product is advertised.
+- `aoa_ffs_mode`: FunctionFS gadget is active, [Android Open Accessory](https://source.android.com/docs/core/interaction/accessories/protocol) ID vendor and product is advertised.
+- `host_mode`: the USB role switch is put in host role, the device acts as USB host.
 
